{:edb/db-before-t 2585 :edb/db-after-t 2586 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535898 50 #inst "2026-10-15T05:25:27.598Z" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186054027 1078 #edb/ref 17592186054031 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186054626 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538925 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186054626 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538949 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186054626 1038 #edb/ref 17592186539001 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186054627 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538969 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186054627 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538971 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186054627 1038 #edb/ref 17592186539026 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186054704 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538930 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186054836 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538937 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186055465 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186055465 1071 "Evaluate $(-\\frac{1}{3}) \\div ((-4)^{3} \\div 8)$." 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186055465 1071 "Evaluate $(-\\frac{1}{3})÷((-4)^{3}÷8).$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186055465 1074 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, consider the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)^{3}÷8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying PEMDAS to this expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)^{3}÷8 & = \\\\ (-64)÷8 & = \\\\ \\frac{(-64)}{8} & = \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\frac{1}{3})÷((-4)^{3}÷8) & = \\\\ (-\\frac{1}{3})÷(-8) & = \\\\ (-\\frac{1}{3})\\times (-\\frac{1}{8}) & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\times 1}{3\\times 8} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186058506 1075 #edb/ref 17592186058507 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186058506 1075 #edb/ref 17592186058508 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186058506 1075 #edb/ref 17592186058509 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186058506 1075 #edb/ref 17592186058510 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186058506 1075 #edb/ref 17592186058511 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186058506 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538319 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186058506 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538550 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186058506 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538667 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186058506 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538737 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186058506 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538782 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186058506 1078 #edb/ref 17592186538319 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186059869 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538950 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186059869 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538979 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186059870 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538929 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186059870 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538961 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186060314 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186060314 1071 "Owen enrolled in an online course that has $12$ topics. He completed $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the remaining topics every week. Which expression gives approximately  the number of topics that were incomplete after $3$ weeks?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186060314 1071 "Owen enrolled in an online course that has $12$ topics. He completed $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the remaining topics every week. Which expression gives the number of topics that were incomplete after $3$ weeks?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186060314 1074 "If the number of incomplete topics decreased by $\\frac{1}{6}$ every week, then every week $\\frac{5}{6}$ of the topics remained incomplete.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ week, $12\\cdot (\\frac{5}{6})$ topics were incomplete.\n\n- After $2$ weeks, approximately  $12\\cdot {(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}$ topics were incomplete.\n\n- After $3$ weeks, approximately $12\\cdot {(\\frac{5}{6})}^{3}$ topics were incomplete." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186064290 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538944 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186064290 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538989 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186064387 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538987 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186064387 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538990 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186064388 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538976 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186066832 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066834 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186066832 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538396 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186066832 1078 #edb/ref 17592186066836 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186068176 1075 #edb/ref 17592186068179 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186068176 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538405 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186068176 1078 #edb/ref 17592186068177 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186068188 1078 #edb/ref 17592186068191 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186068597 1071 "What is the result of dividing $\\dfrac 3 x$ by $\\dfrac y {6x^2}$?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186068597 1071 "What is the result of dividing $\\dfrac 3 x$ by $\\dfrac y {6x^2}?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186068597 1074 "To compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x} \\div \\dfrac{y}{6x^2}\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac 3 x \\div \\dfrac y {6x^2} &=\\ \\dfrac 3 x \\cdot \\dfrac {6x^2} y &=\\ \\dfrac {18x^2} {xy}&=\\ \\dfrac {18x^\\cancel{2}} {\\cancel{x}y}&=\\ \\dfrac {18x} {y}& \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the final step, we canceled out a common factor of $x$ in the numerator and denominator." 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186068597 1074 "To compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x} \\div \\dfrac{y}{6x^2},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac 3 x \\div  \\dfrac y {6x^2} &=\\\n\t\t\\dfrac 3 x \\cdot  \\dfrac {6x^2} y &=\\\n\t\t\\dfrac {18x^2} {xy}&=\\\n\t\t\\dfrac {18x^\\cancel{2}} {\\cancel{x}y}&=\\\n\t\t\\dfrac {18x} {y}&\t\t\n\t}\n$$\n\nNote that in the final step, we canceled out a common factor of $x$ in the numerator and denominator." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186068598 1071 "Given that $\\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b}  =x$ and $\\dfrac {1+b}{5b^2} =y ,$ then what is $x \\div y?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186068598 1071 "Given that $\\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b} =x$ and $\\dfrac {1+b}{5b^2} =y$, then what is $x \\div y$?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186068598 1074 "To compute $x \\div y$, i.e.,\n\n$$\n(2a^{2}(1 + b))/(3b) \\mid \\div \\frac{1 + b}{5b^{2}}\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b} \\div \\dfrac{1+b}{5b^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b} \\cdot \\dfrac {5b^2} {1+b} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {2a^2\\cancel{(1+b)}} {3\\cancel{b}} \\cdot \\dfrac {5b^{\\cancel{2}}} {\\cancel{1+b}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {10a^2b} {3} \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186068598 1074 "To compute $x \\div y,$ i.e., \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{a}^{2}(1+b)}{3b} & ÷\\frac{1+b}{5{b}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b}  \\div  \\dfrac{1+b}{5b^2}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {5b^2} {1+b}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {2a^2\\cancel{(1+b)}} {3\\cancel{b}}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {5b^{\\cancel{2}}} {\\cancel{1+b}}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {10a^2b} {3}  \n\t}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186068604 1071 "What is the result of dividing $\\dfrac 3 x$ by $\\dfrac {5}{y}$?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186068604 1071 "What is the result of dividing $\\dfrac 3 x$ by $\\dfrac {5}{y}?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186068604 1074 "To compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x} \\div \\dfrac{5}{y}\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac 3 x \\div \\dfrac 5 y&=\\ \\dfrac 3 x \\cdot \\dfrac y 5&=\\ \\dfrac {3y} {5x} \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186068604 1074 "To compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x} \\div \\dfrac{5}{y},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac 3 x \\div  \\dfrac 5 y&=\\\n\t\t\\dfrac 3 x \\cdot  \\dfrac y 5&=\\\n\t\t\\dfrac {3y} {5x}\t\t\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186068605 1071 "Given that $\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  =x$ and $\\dfrac {2a} {14b^2}=y,$ then what is $x \\div y?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186068605 1071 "Given that $\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b} =x$ and $\\dfrac {2a} {14b^2}=y$, then what is $x \\div y$?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186068605 1074 "To compute\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\n\t\t\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  &\\div  \\dfrac {2a} {14b^2},\n\t}\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  \\div  \\dfrac {2a} {14b^2}&=\\\n\t\t\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  \\cdot \\dfrac {14b^2} {2a}&=\\\n\t\t\\dfrac {2a(a^2 - 5)} {7b}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {2b \\cdot 7b} {2a}&=\\\n\t\t\\dfrac {\\cancel{2a}(a^2 - 5)} {\\cancel{7b}}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {2b \\cdot \\cancel{7b}} {\\cancel{2a}}&=\\\n\t\t\\dfrac {2b(a^2 - 5)} 1 &=\\\n\t\t2a^2b - 10b &\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186068605 1074 "To compute\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b} &\\div \\dfrac {2a} {14b^2}, \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b} \\div \\dfrac {2a} {14b^2}&=\\ \\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b} \\cdot \\dfrac {14b^2} {2a}&=\\ \\dfrac {2a(a^2 - 5)} {7b} \\cdot \\dfrac {2b \\cdot 7b} {2a}&=\\ \\dfrac {\\cancel{2a}(a^2 - 5)} {\\cancel{7b}} \\cdot \\dfrac {2b \\cdot \\cancel{7b}} {\\cancel{2a}}&=\\ \\dfrac {2b(a^2 - 5)} 1 \\\\ &= 2a^2b - 10b & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186068763 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538955 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186068764 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538965 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186068805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538931 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186068967 1050 480.0 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186069277 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186069277 1071 "$\\frac{12{b}^{2}}{5}÷\\frac{36b}{20}=$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186069277 1071 "What is $\\frac{12b^{2}}{5} \\div \\frac{36b}{20}$?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186069277 1074 "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{12{b}^{2}}{5}÷\\frac{36b}{20},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12{b}^{2}}{5}÷\\frac{36b}{20} & = \\\\ \\frac{12{b}^{2}}{5}\\cdot \\frac{20}{36b} & = \\\\ \\frac{12b\\cdot b}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5\\cdot 4}{12b\\cdot 3} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{12b}\\cdot b}{\\cancel{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{5}\\cdot 4}{\\cancel{12b}\\cdot 3} & = \\\\ \\frac{4b}{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186069501 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186069501 1071 "$\\frac{3xz}{4}÷\\frac{2z}{5}=$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186069501 1071 "What is $\\frac{3xz}{4} \\div \\frac{2z}{5}$?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186069501 1074 "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{3xz}{4}÷\\frac{2z}{5},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3xz}{4}÷\\frac{2z}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{3xz}{4}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2z} & = \\\\ \\frac{15xz}{8z} & = \\\\ \\frac{15x\\cancel{z}}{8\\cancel{z}} & = \\\\ \\frac{15x}{8} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the final step, we canceled out a common factor of $z$ in the numerator and denominator." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186069503 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186069503 1071 "$\\frac{5b}{7}÷\\frac{2}{a}=$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186069503 1071 "What is $\\frac{5b}{7} \\div \\frac{2}{a}$?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186069503 1074 "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{5b}{7}÷\\frac{2}{a},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5b}{7}÷\\frac{2}{a} & = \\\\ \\frac{5b}{7}\\cdot \\frac{a}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{5ab}{14} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186069973 1064 "To divide one rational expression by another, we multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor. Just like with fractions!\n\nFor example, let's divide $x$ by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{y}\n$$\n\n$$\nx \\div \\dfrac{3}{y}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor. The divisor is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{y}\n$$\n\nand to get the reciprocal, we just flip the fraction upside down:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y}{3}\n$$\n\nMultiplying by the reciprocal of the divisor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx \\div \\frac{3}{y} &=  \\\\\nx \\cdot \\frac{y}{3} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{xy}{3} \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples!" 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186069973 1064 "To divide one rational expression by another, we multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor. Just like with fractions!\n\nFor example, let's divide $x$ by $\\dfrac{3}{y}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx \\div \\dfrac{3}{y}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor. The divisor is $\\dfrac{3}{y},$ and to get the reciprocal, we just flip the fraction upside down: $\\dfrac{y}{3}.$ Multiplying by the reciprocal of the divisor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x÷\\frac{3}{y} & = \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{xy}{3} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples!" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186073895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538941 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186073898 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538992 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186082373 1078 #edb/ref 17592186082374 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186083277 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538981 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186083279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538997 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186083279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538999 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186083907 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186083907 1071 "Consider the positive infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $18$ and a third term equal to $2$. What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186083907 1071 "Consider the positive infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $18$ and a third term equal to $2.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186083907 1074 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=18$ and ${a}_{3}=2.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3-1} & =\\frac{2}{18} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have taken the positive square root since we're told that the sequence is positive.\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186096678 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538966 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186100884 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538973 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186104428 1075 #edb/ref 17592186104429 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186104428 1075 #edb/ref 17592186104430 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186104428 1075 #edb/ref 17592186104431 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186104428 1075 #edb/ref 17592186104432 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186104428 1075 #edb/ref 17592186104433 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186104428 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538266 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186104428 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538404 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186104428 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538609 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186104428 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538735 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186104428 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538750 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186104428 1078 #edb/ref 17592186538735 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186104842 1078 #edb/ref 17592186104845 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186105901 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538963 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186105920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186539019 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186106380 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186106380 1071 "The $10$th and $11$th terms of an arithmetic sequence are $30$ and $33$, respectively. Determine the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186106380 1071 "The $10$th and $11$th terms of an arithmetic sequence are $30$ and $33,$ respectively. Determine the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186106380 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nSince the terms ${a}_{10}=30$ and ${a}_{11}=33$ are consecutive, we can subtract them to find the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{11}-{a}_{10} \\\\  & =33-30 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{10}=30,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{10}$ is $9$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{10} & ={a}_{1}+9d \\\\ 30 & ={a}_{1}+9(3) \\\\ 30 & ={a}_{1}+27 \\\\ 30-27 & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =3+(n-1)(3) \\\\  & =3+3n-3 \\\\  & =3n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186106449 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186106449 1071 "Find the $31$st term of an arithmetic sequence given that the $22$nd term is $76$ and the $6$th term is $-4$." 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186106449 1071 "Find the $31$st term of an arithmetic sequence given that the $22$nd term is $76$ and the $6$th term is $-4.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186106449 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{22}=76$ and ${a}_{6}=-4$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{22}-{a}_{6}}{22-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{76-(-4)}{22-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{80}{16} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{6}=-4,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{6}$ is $5$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}+5d \\\\ -4 & ={a}_{1}+5\\cdot 5 \\\\ -4 & ={a}_{1}+25 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-29+(n-1)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-34+5n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find the $31$st term by substituting $n=31$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31} & =-34+5(31) \\\\  & =-34+155 \\\\  & =121\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186107195 1075 #edb/ref 17592186107196 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186107195 1075 #edb/ref 17592186107197 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186107195 1075 #edb/ref 17592186107198 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186107195 1075 #edb/ref 17592186107199 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186107195 1075 #edb/ref 17592186107200 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186107195 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538332 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186107195 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538347 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186107195 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538390 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186107195 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538413 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186107195 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538748 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186107195 1078 #edb/ref 17592186538413 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186107207 1075 #edb/ref 17592186107208 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186107207 1075 #edb/ref 17592186107209 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186107207 1075 #edb/ref 17592186107210 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186107207 1075 #edb/ref 17592186107211 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186107207 1075 #edb/ref 17592186107212 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186107207 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538340 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186107207 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538463 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186107207 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538572 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [175