us solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-1) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-2-1) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2(-2)+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-3) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\log (-3)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538956 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538985 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186539009 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115214 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538942 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115214 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538984 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115214 1038 #edb/ref 17592186539000 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115214 1038 #edb/ref 17592186539022 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115215 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538932 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115215 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538951 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115443 1050 480.0 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115640 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115640 1071 "Given that $\\log (3x - 2) = \\log (8 - 2x)$, what is the value of $x$?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186115640 1071 "Given that $\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x-2)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(8-2x),$ what is the value of $x?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115640 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(8-2x) \\\\ 3x-2 & =8-2x \\\\ 5x & =10 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(3(2)-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(8-2(2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is a valid solution." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115653 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115653 1071 "If $\\ln (x^{2} - 9) = \\ln (7)$, then what is the value of $x$?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186115653 1071 "If $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-9)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7),$ then what is the value of $x?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115653 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-9) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ {x}^{2}-9 & =7 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=4$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-9) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(16-9) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=4$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-4$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-9) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((-4)^{2}-9) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(16-9) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-4$ is a valid solution.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"$x=\\pm 4.$\"" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115654 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115654 1071 "Solve the equation $\\ln (2x^{2} - 2) = \\ln (3x)$." 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186115654 1071 "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x).$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115654 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2 & =3x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3x-2 & =0 \\\\ (2x+1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2},2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(-\\frac{3}{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not valid because $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-\\frac{3}{2})$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot {(2)}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot (2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$x=2$ only.\"" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115941 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115941 1071 "Solve $\\log (2x) = \\log (3x + 2)$ for $x$." 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186115941 1071 "Solve $\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x+2)$ for $x.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115941 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x+2) \\\\ 2x & =3x+2 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x+2) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(2(-2)) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(3(-2)+2) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\operatorname{log}⁡(-4)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186116482 1064 "To solve an equation that has logarithms with the same base on both sides, we can exponentiate both sides of the equation by the base. For example, consider the equation\n\n$$\n\\log_3(2x-4)=\\log_3(x+3)\n$$\n\nSince both sides of the equation are represented as logarithms of the same base ($3$), we can exponentiate both sides of the equation by $3$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^{\\log_{3} (2x - 4)} &= 3^{\\log_{3} (x + 3)} \\\\\n2x - 4 &= x + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - 4 &= x + 3 \\\\\nx &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=7$ is the solution.\n\n**Caution:** Remember that, when solving logarithmic equations, it is sometimes possible to get *extraneous* solutions that don't actually satisfy the original equation. We can't take a logarithm of zero or a negative number.\n\nSo, once we've found the solutions, we need to substitute them back into the original equation and check that the arguments of the logarithms are positive. Substituting $x=7$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{3} (2x - 4) &= ^(?)\\log_{3} (x + 3) \\\\\n\\log_{3} (2 \\cdot 7 - 4) &= ^(?)\\log_{3} (7 + 3) \\\\\n\\log_{3} (10) &= \\log_{3} (10).✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=7$ is a valid solution." 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186116482 1064 "To solve an equation that has logarithms with the same base on both sides, we can exponentiate both sides of the equation by the base. For example, consider the equation\n\n$$\n\\log_3(2x-4)=\\log_3(x+3).\n$$\n\nSince both sides of the equation are represented as logarithms of the same base ($3$), we can exponentiate both sides of the equation by $3.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(2x-4)} & ={3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(x+3)} \\\\ 2x-4 & =x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4 & =x+3 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=7$ is the solution.\n\n**Caution:** Remember that, when solving logarithmic equations, it is sometimes possible to get *extraneous* solutions that don't actually satisfy the original equation. We can't take a logarithm of zero or a negative number.\n\nSo, once we've found the solutions, we need to substitute them back into the original equation and check that the arguments of the logarithms are positive. Substituting $x=7$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(2x-4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{3}⁡(x+3) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(2\\cdot 7-4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{3}⁡(7+3) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(10) & ={log}_{3}⁡(10).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=7$ is a valid solution." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186116523 1064 "In the previous example, you may have noticed that for the logarithmic equation\n\n$$\n\\log_3 (2x-4) = \\log_3 (x+3)\n$$\n\nwe were able to \"cancel out\" the logarithms and equate the arguments:\n\n$$\n2x-4 = x+3\n$$\n\nThis result holds in general. Whenever the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments.\n\n**Caution:** This trick only works when the logarithms have the same base. The equation must take the form\n\n$$\n\\log_n f(x) = \\log_n g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the base of *both* logarithms and $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are the arguments of the logarithms." 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186116523 1064 "In the previous example, you may have noticed that for the logarithmic equation\n\n$$\n\\log_3 (2x-4) = \\log_3 (x+3),\n$$\n\nwe were able to \"cancel out\" the logarithms and equate the arguments:\n\n$$\n2x-4 = x+3\n$$\n\nThis result holds in general. Whenever the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments.\n\n**Caution:** This trick only works when the logarithms have the same base. The equation must take the form\n\n$$\n\\log_n f(x) = \\log_n g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the base of *both* logarithms and $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are the arguments of the logarithms." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186129111 1038 #edb/ref 17592186539005 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186129129 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538983 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186129178 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538936 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186133964 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538948 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186135427 1075 #edb/ref 17592186135428 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186135427 1075 #edb/ref 17592186135429 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186135427 1075 #edb/ref 17592186135430 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186135427 1075 #edb/ref 17592186135431 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186135427 1075 #edb/ref 17592186135432 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186135427 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538282 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186135427 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538329 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186135427 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538391 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186135427 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538448 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186135427 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538742 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186135427 1078 #edb/ref 17592186538448 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186135493 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538323 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186135493 1078 #edb/ref 17592186538323 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186138389 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538958 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186138389 1038 #edb/ref 17592186539011 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186138395 1038 #edb/ref 17592186539012 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186138698 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186138698 1071 "The shoulder height of a moose $h$ measures $2.1\\,\\text{m}$ to the nearest tenth of a meter. Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186138698 1071 "The shoulder height of a moose $h$ measures $2.1m$ to the nearest tenth of a meter. Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement." 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186138698 1074 "Our measurement is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{m}.$ So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.1\\,\\text{m}}{2}=0.05\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{lower bound} & =2.1\\,\\text{m}-0.05\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =2.05\\,\\text{m} \\\\ \\text{upper bound} & =2.1\\,\\text{m}+0.05\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =2.15\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $h$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2.05\\,\\text{m}\\le h<2.15\\,\\text{m}.\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186138737 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186138737 1071 "The dimensions of a rectangular floor are $12\\,\\text{m}\\times 3\\,\\text{m}$, where each dimension is measured to the nearest $1\\,\\text{m}.$ Rounded to the nearest square meter, what is the smallest possible area of the floor?\n\nTo the nearest square meter, the smallest possible area is\n{{field-1}}${\\text{m}}^{2}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186138737 1071 "The dimensions of a rectangular floor are $12m \\cdot 3m$, where each dimension is measured to the nearest $1m$. Rounded to the nearest square meter, what is the smallest possible area of the floor?To the nearest square meter, the smallest possible area is\n{{field-1}} $m^{2}$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186138737 1074 "Each dimension is given to the nearest $1\\,\\text{m}.$ So, the greatest possible error of these measurements is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1\\,\\text{m}}{2}=0.5\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the *smallest* possible area of the container. Therefore, we require the *lower bound* of each dimension.\n\nThe lower bounds for each dimension are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 12\\,\\text{m}-0.5\\,\\text{m}=11.5\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & 3\\,\\text{m}-0.5\\,\\text{m}=2.5\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible area of the container is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =11.5\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.5\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =28.75\\,{\\text{m}}^{2} \\\\  & ≈29\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest square meter." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186145112 1078 #edb/ref 17592186145113 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186147330 1074 "The cube has $6$ square faces:\n\n![](images/e8/e86115935aa177dcab50125df50bf9df825e9a47cf5c94d9fd28f587943e3b5a.png)" 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186147330 1074 "The cube has $6$ square faces: \n\n![](images/e8/e86115935aa177dcab50125df50bf9df825e9a47cf5c94d9fd28f587943e3b5a.png)" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186147331 1071 "How many vertices does the rectangular prism shown below have?\n\n![](images/dc/dc430f51134ef0b94a2e8c0932816f80f64321601a3b815f47cb67acd8f028a1.png)" 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186147331 1071 "How many vertices does the rectangular prism shown below have? \n\n![](images/dc/dc430f51134ef0b94a2e8c0932816f80f64321601a3b815f47cb67acd