576 1071 "Consider the function $f: R^{3} → R$ defined by $f(x) = x^{T}x$. What is the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186204576 1071 "Consider the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{T}x.$ What is the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186204576 1074 "The total derivative of a scalar-valued function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ with respect to a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is the row vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{1\\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function \n\n- acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3},$ and\n\n- outputs a scalar $f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3})\\in R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{3}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} & 2{x}_{2} & 2{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{1\\times 3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also write this as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=2{x}^{T}.\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186204577 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186204577 1071 "For $x, y, z ∈ R^{2}$, consider the following functions:\n\n$y(x) = AxwhereA = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}; z(y) = \\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}^{2} \\\\ y_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}; f(z) = CzwhereC = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhat is $\\frac{d}{dx}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at $x_{0} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186204577 1071 "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186204577 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2(1) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2(3)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}28 & 44 \\\\ 18 & 24\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186204579 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186204579 1071 "Consider the function $f$, defined as\n$f(x) = \\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat is the shape of the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186204579 1071 "Consider the function $f,$ defined as \n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the shape of the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186204579 1074 "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:R\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a scalar $x$ is the column vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂x}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{m\\times 1}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function \n\n- acts on a scalar $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- outputs a vector $f(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the total derivative will be a column vector of shape $2\\times 1.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186204591 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186204591 1071 "For $x, y, z ∈ R^{2}$, consider the following functions:\n\n$y(x) = AxwhereA = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}; z(y) = \\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}y_{2} \\\\ y_{1} + y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}; f(z) = CzwhereC = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhat is $\\frac{d}{dx}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at $x_{0} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186204591 1071 "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186204591 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{1} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 13 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186233039 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538917 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186233039 1071 "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{log}(x-2)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}(256).$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186233039 1071 "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}⁡(256).$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186233039 1074 "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}(256) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}({256}^{1/4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}(4) \\\\ x-2 & =4 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=6$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}(6-2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}({256}^{1/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}(256).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=6$ is a valid solution." 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186233039 1074 "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}⁡(256) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({256}^{1/4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) \\\\ x-2 & =4 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=6$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(6-2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({256}^{1/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}⁡(256).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=6$ is a valid solution." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186233189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538860 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186233699 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538906 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186233699 1071 "Given that ${log}_{2}(3x+1)={log}_{2}(5-x),$ what is the value of $x?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 false] [17592186233699 1071 "Given that ${log}_{2}⁡(3x+1)={log}_{2}⁡(5-x),$ what is the value of $x?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186233699 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(3x+1) & ={log}_{2}(5-x) \\\\ 3x+1 & =5-x \\\\ 4x & =4 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(3(1)+1) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}(5-1) \\\\ {log}_{2}(4) & ={log}_{2}(4)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a valid solution." 13194139535898 1759218