5898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538922 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538561 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538922 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538773 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538922 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538922 1077 "selection" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538922 1078 #edb/ref 17592186538561 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538922 1079 #uuid "fd827dea-e1f9-5732-9f7b-3296383b4adf" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538923 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538326 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538923 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538433 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538923 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538551 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538923 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538681 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538923 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538722 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538923 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538923 1077 "selection" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538923 1078 #edb/ref 17592186538681 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538923 1079 #uuid "fe2698ca-7e20-5c2e-95df-9f69c77c58a2" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538746 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538924 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538924 1077 "field-1" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538924 1078 #edb/ref 17592186538746 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538924 1079 #uuid "ffbb06e8-1787-59c8-b541-824a6fbe128b" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538925 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538888 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538925 1069 "q-52025" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538925 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538925 1071 "Evaluate $|4y+6{|}^{3}$ for $y=-2.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538925 1072 #uuid "00b7055c-6f6b-518a-86a9-ac2dadfdf918" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538925 1074 "We substitute $y=-2$ and then evaluate the absolute value before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|4y+6{|}^{3} & = \\\\ |4(-2)+6{|}^{3} & = \\\\ |-8+6{|}^{3} & = \\\\ |-2{|}^{3} & = \\\\ {2}^{3} & = \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538853 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538926 1069 "q-97750" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538926 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538926 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{(x+1)\\sqrt{9-(3x+3)^{2}}}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538926 1072 #uuid "02d58382-8176-5a03-b109-135b67345884" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538926 1074 "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{(x+1)\\sqrt{9-(3x+3)^{2}}} & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{(x+1)\\sqrt{9-9{(x+1)}^{2}}}=\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)\\sqrt{1-(x+1)^{2}}}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=x+1.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)\\sqrt{1-(x+1)^{2}}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(x+1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538927 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538908 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538927 1069 "q-93180" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538927 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538927 1071 "![](images/4b/4b5055d5290bfefa599d5fa357b76a3ccff05f9b0330c6610ce77deba949e34f.png)\n\nHow many vertices does the heptagonal pyramid shown above have?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538927 1072 #uuid "0a370d7b-dd7e-58da-9b48-e4a346c93a21" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538927 1074 "Vertices are points that connect the straight line segments (edges) of a polyhedron. \n\nThe heptagonal pyramid has $8$ vertices in total, as shown below.\n\n![](images/94/94b52de8784cf5294913cd558d07abbd21446e7ab6b7f70923301ca94040fba0.png)" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538928 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538861 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538928 1069 "q-107260" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538928 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538928 1071 "Find the volume of the cube whose sides each have length $7\\,\\text{cm}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538928 1072 #uuid "0ed72443-d0d2-56d4-ac8e-61b5fbb5185e" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538928 1074 "The volume $V$ of a cube can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nV={s}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the side length of the cube.\n\nSubstituting $s=7\\,\\text{cm}$ into the formula, we get \n\n$$\nV=(7\\,\\text{cm})^{3}=343\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538929 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538889 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538929 1069 "q-61965" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538929 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538929 1071 "A soccer tournament began with $64$ teams. The number of teams reduced by $\\frac{1}{2}$ after every round. How many teams remained after $6$ rounds?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538929 1072 #uuid "11af995c-4c5e-50de-8182-ca4e90fd1ecf" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538929 1074 "There were $64$ initially, and this number decreased by $\\frac{1}{2}$ every round. \n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ round, there were $64\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})$ teams remaining.\n\n- After $2$ rounds, there were $64\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}$ teams remaining.\n\n- After $3$ rounds, there were $64\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}$ teams  remaining.\n\n- $…$\n\nRepeating this pattern, we see that there were $64\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}$ teams remaining after $6$ rounds." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538834 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538930 1069 "q-2893" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538930 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538930 1071 "${z}^{4}\\cdot {z}^{3}=$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538930 1072 #uuid "16bce723-03c6-53a5-b2a1-c812d455c8ee" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538930 1074 "We add the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{4}\\cdot {z}^{3} & = \\\\ {z}^{4+3} & = \\\\ {z}^{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538902 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538931 1069 "q-100082" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538931 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538931 1071 "$\\frac{ab+3b}{5a+15}=$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538931 1072 #uuid "1a5f882d-4cbf-515d-b127-b49f03d6e601" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538931 1074 "We factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{ab+3b}{5a+15} & =\\frac{b(a+3)}{5(a+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{b\\cancel{(a+3)}}{5\\cancel{(a+3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{b}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538863 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538932 1069 "q-27743" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538932 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538932 1071 "Given that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x-4)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+2),$ what is the value of $x?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538932 1072 #uuid "1e549fe4-3be0-5aa2-9b34-f060b6793b1c" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538932 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x-4) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+2) \\\\ 7x-4 & =5x+2 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=3$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7(3)-4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5(3)+2) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(17) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(17)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=3$ is a valid solution." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538933 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538862 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538933 1069 "q-107299" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538933 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538933 1071 "Three identical cubic tanks can hold a combined total of $375\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}$ of water.  Find the side length of a single tank." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538933 1072 #uuid "20052714-006e-5cb8-9e42-f681fe3b072f" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538933 1074 "First, we find the volume of one tank:  \n\n$$\n\\frac{375\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}}{3}=125\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the side length of a single tank.\n\nSince the volume of a cube is \n\n$$\nV={s}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the length of a side, we can find $s$ as follows:\n\n$$\ns=\\sqrt[3]{V}=\\sqrt[3]{125}=5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of a side is $5\\,\\text{m}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538934 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538842 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538934 1069 "q-269858" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538934 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538934 1071 "Consider a function $f:{R}^{3\\times 3}\\to {R}^{3}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(A)=A[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven that ${a}_{ij}$ denotes the entry of $A$ in the intersection of the $i$th row and $j$th column, we have\n\n$\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{a}_{31}}=${{field-1}}." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538934 1072 #uuid "20f2946c-2b05-548e-8d4f-c195e7f5a6e5" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538934 1074 "We can write the function $f$ as\n\n$$\nf(A)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & {a}_{13} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & {a}_{23} \\\\ {a}_{31} & {a}_{32} & {a}_{33}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{11}+3{a}_{12}-{a}_{13} \\\\ 2{a}_{21}+3{a}_{22}-{a}_{23} \\\\ 2{a}_{31}+3{a}_{32}-{a}_{33}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{a}_{31}}=\\frac{∂}{∂{a}_{31}}(2{a}_{31}+3{a}_{32}-{a}_{33})=2.\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538935 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538829 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538935 1069 "q-132437" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538935 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538935 1071 "Given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}),$ what is $\\text{Var}[X]?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538935 1072 #uuid "224b72f4-7dda-5cd7-89ac-9950a44cc628" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538935 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}),$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538936 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538851 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538936 1069 "q-129553" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538936 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538936 1071 "At which points on the surface $z=8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538936 1072 #uuid "278532bd-d5b2-5391-ac62-5dd37f8bae10" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538936 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =24{x}^{2}-12y \\\\  & =12(2{x}^{2}-y) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =-12x+3{y}^{2} \\\\  & =3({y}^{2}-4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨12(2{x}^{2}-y),\\,3({y}^{2}-4x),\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}12(2{x}^{2}-y)=0 \\\\ 3({y}^{2}-4x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=2{x}^{2}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({(2{x}^{2})}^{2}-4x) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{4}-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{3}-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions of the last equation are $x=0,1.$ So, we have two cases.\n\n- For $x=0,$ we have \n\n$$\ny=2({0}^{2})=0.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=8({0}^{3})-12(0)(0)+{0}^{3}+1=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our first point is $(0,0,1).$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we have \n\n$$\ny=2({1}^{2})=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ and $y=2$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=8({1}^{3})-12(1)(2)+{2}^{3}+1=-7.\n$$\n\nSo, our second point is $(1,2,-7).$\n\nTherefore, the points where the tangent plane is horizontal are $(0,0,1)$ and $(1,2,-7).$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538924 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538937 1069 "q-175591" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538937 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538937 1071 "If $-\\frac{x}{12}=-\\frac{1}{6},$ then $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538937 1072 #uuid "2c9f2909-7e69-5612-95f1-1f03b540b4de" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538937 1074 "First, let's remove the negative sign from the variable. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{x}{12} & =-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{12}) & =(-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\ \\frac{x}{12} & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the equation above, $x$ is being divided by $12.$ To isolate $x$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $12.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $12$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{12} & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 12\\cdot \\frac{x}{12} & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{12x}{12} & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{12}x}{\\cancel{12}} & =2 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538938 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538887 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538938 1069 "q-144246" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538938 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538938 1071 "Simplify the expression $-6q-(q-9).$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538938 1072 #uuid "2eb696c5-dbcf-5ba0-8d82-82e64e6ac266" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538938 1074 "First, we remove the parentheses by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n-6q-q+9\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6q-q+9 & = \\\\ (-6q-q)+9 & = \\\\ -7q+9 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538939 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538872 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538939 1069 "q-50922" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538939 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538939 1071 "What is the sum of the series $\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{5}}{5!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}{\\pi }^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\,?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538939 1072 #uuid "2f5058c8-bee4-550a-9863-d36cd2227082" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538939 1074 "We recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\pi $ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi  & =\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{5}}{5!}-\\frac{{\\pi }^{7}}{7!}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}{\\pi }^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯ \\\\ 0 & =\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{5}}{5!}-\\frac{{\\pi }^{7}}{7!}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}{\\pi }^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $0.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538940 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538869 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538940 1069 "q-107285" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538940 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538940 1071 "Six foam cubes has a combined volume of $162\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.$ Find the side length of a single cube." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538940 1072 #uuid "303cc00a-f8f7-5cb0-b148-e5fe2714f366" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538940 1074 "First, we find the volume of one foam cube:\n\n$$\n\\frac{162\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}}{6}=27\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the side length of one foam cube.\n\nSince the volume of a cube is \n\n$$\nV={s}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the length of a side, we can find $s$ as follows:\n\n$$\ns=\\sqrt[3]{V}=\\sqrt[3]{27}=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of a side is $3\\,\\text{in}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538941 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538858 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538941 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538882 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538941 1069 "q-186859" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538941 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538941 1071 "Complete the ratio table below.\n\n| $7$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} | $6$ |\n| {{field-2}} | $20$ |" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538941 1072 #uuid "321e819d-21c6-5c8d-a9d3-36abfa3818e4" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538941 1074 "We start with the ratio $7:2$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{2}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry ($7$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry ($2$).\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n- To create a denominator of $6,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{7\\times 3}{2\\times 3}=\\frac{21}{6}\n$$\n\n- To create a denominator of $20,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $10:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{7\\times 10}{2\\times 10}=\\frac{70}{20}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n| $7$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $21$ | $6$ |\n| $70$ | $20$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538942 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538921 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538942 1069 "q-79872" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538942 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538942 1071 "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x+4).$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538942 1072 #uuid "325f155d-1090-569d-9381-79bbdc699aa8" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538942 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x+4) \\\\ {x}^{2}-2 & =-x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-6 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=-3$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡((-3)^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-(-3)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-3$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡((2)^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-(2)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-3,2.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538943 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538818 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538943 1069 "q-107266" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538943 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538943 1071 "![](images/ec/ec35ecf376e5f5108d379ee965b8ea7ce32223050fa09e8d90ad2cf769853894.png)\n\nThe volume of the cube above is $1\\,728\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ What is the length of its sides?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538943 1072 #uuid "3512c6b0-1995-511f-b3bb-e6f73ca555fc" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538943 1074 "Since the volume of a cube is \n\n$$\nV={s}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the length of a side, we can find $s$ as follows:\n\n$$\ns=\\sqrt[3]{V}=\\sqrt[3]{1\\,728}=12\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of a side is $12\\,\\text{cm}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538944 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538822 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538944 1069 "q-121678" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538944 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538944 1071 "![](images/b1/b18b1e3a686f70a4c583324ddc253e8d334f63f017639bd47f6f483d42582801.png)\n\nAn inverted conical dispenser has a diameter of $100\\,\\text{in}$ and a depth of $110\\,\\text{in}.$ If a liquid is flowing out of the vertex of container at a rate of $9\\,000\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/h}$, how fast is the height of the liquid dropping when the liquid is $60\\,\\text{in}$ deep?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538944 1072 #uuid "371ac971-2ba8-5a04-9b8d-bbb2a2347b46" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538944 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=110\\,\\text{in}$ the height of the container, and $R=\\frac{100}{2}=50\\,\\text{in}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-9\\,000\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=60\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{50}{110} \\\\ r & =\\frac{5h}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{5h}{11})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{363}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{25}{363}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{25}{121}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9\\,000\\pi  & =\\frac{25}{121}\\pi (60)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -9\\,000\\pi  & =\\frac{90\\,000}{121}\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-12.1\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water height is decreasing at a rate of $12.1\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538874 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538945 1069 "q-350677" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538945 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538945 1071 "What is the sum of the series $1-\\frac{{(3\\pi /2)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(3\\pi /2)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,(3\\pi /2)^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\,?$\n\nThe sum of the series is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538945 1072 #uuid "38c8627e-3005-599a-a37a-fb4e0aa52cad" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538945 1074 "We recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =1-\\frac{{(3\\pi /2)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(3\\pi /2)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,(3\\pi /2)^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ 0 & =1-\\frac{{(3\\pi /2)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(3\\pi /2)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,(3\\pi /2)^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $0.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538824 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538946 1069 "q-103114" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538946 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538946 1071 "$1-\\frac{25}{2!}+\\frac{625}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{5}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯=$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538946 1072 #uuid "3922f3d0-9ec0-55ed-b2ff-7790813baa5b" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538946 1074 "We recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5 & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{5}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{5}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡5 & =1-\\frac{25}{2!}+\\frac{625}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{5}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡5.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538947 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538814 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538947 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538843 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538947 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538913 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538947 1069 "q-269808" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538947 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538947 1071 "Consider the functions $y(x)=Ax$ and $f(y)=By,$ where $x\\in {R}^{2},y\\in {R}^{3},$ and the matrices $A$ and $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[0,\\,1]^{T}?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538947 1072 #uuid "3c639ae0-88c7-55de-8084-a7f89ff7f5c1" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538947 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{3}$ by multiplying it by $B$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}=B.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =BA \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 15 \\\\ 11 & 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538948 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538881 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538948 1069 "q-175877" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538948 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538948 1071 "If $r=1,$ then $\\frac{7r}{15}+\\frac{6}{5r}=${{field-1}}." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538948 1072 #uuid "3d20e092-7a52-54cf-904a-8aa79d562ce0" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538948 1074 "We replace $r$ with $1$ in the expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7r}{15}+\\frac{6}{5r} & = \\\\ \\frac{7\\cdot 1}{15}+\\frac{6}{5\\cdot 1} & = \\\\ \\frac{7}{15}+\\frac{6}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{7}{15}+\\frac{18}{15} & = \\\\ \\frac{7+18}{15} & = \\\\ \\frac{25}{15} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538949 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538846 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538949 1069 "q-131806" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538949 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538949 1071 "If $x=2$ then $|3-{2}^{x}|+4=$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538949 1072 #uuid "3e848dbc-438d-5d9d-8de6-41c96328b31b" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538949 1074 "We substitute $x=2$ and then evaluate the absolute value before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3-{2}^{x}|+4 & = \\\\ |3-{2}^{2}|+4 & = \\\\ |3-4|+4 & = \\\\ |-1|+4 & = \\\\ 1+4 & = \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538866 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538950 1069 "q-63873" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538950 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538950 1071 "A park is estimated to have $11\\,000$ rabbits. If their population decreases by $2%$ every year, what will the population of rabbits be approximately in ten years?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538950 1072 #uuid "3f3966bd-9f89-5978-a943-b86356ea0f72" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538950 1074 "The number $2%$ (expressed as a decimal) is equal to $0.02.$ Therefore, a decrease of $2%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of:\n\n$$\n1-0.02=0.98\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ year, there will be $11\\,000\\cdot (0.98)$ rabbits.\n\n- After $2$ years, there will be approximately $11\\,000\\cdot (0.98)^{2}$ rabbits.\n\n- After $3$ years, there will be approximately $11\\,000\\cdot (0.98)^{3}$ rabbits.\n\n- $…$\n\nRepeating this pattern, we see that there will be approximately $11\\,000\\cdot (0.98)^{10}$ rabbits in $10$ years." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538951 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538894 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538951 1069 "q-27725" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538951 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538951 1071 "Solve $\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x-1)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(6x+9)$ for $x.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538951 1072 #uuid "41f4ba90-bf6f-5f13-ae98-77405268cba9" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538951 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x-1) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(6x+9) \\\\ 2x-1 & =6x+9 \\\\ 4x & =-10 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2(-\\frac{5}{2})-1) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(6(-\\frac{5}{2})+9) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-6) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\operatorname{log}⁡(-6)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538952 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538904 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538952 1069 "q-97907" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538952 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538952 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{6x-{x}^{2}-5}\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538952 1072 #uuid "42211531-3fd4-52dd-adb0-73d677948d1f" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538952 1074 "First, we complete the square for the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-{x}^{2}-5 & =-({x}^{2}-6x+5) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}+5) \\\\  & =-((x-3)^{2}-4) \\\\  & =4-(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator of our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{6x-{x}^{2}-5}\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{4-(x-3)^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{1}{2}(x-3))}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{1}{2}(x-3).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{1}{2}(x-3))}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x-3))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{x-3}{2}|>1.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538953 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538923 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538953 1069 "q-97313" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538953 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538953 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{(6x-4)\\sqrt{9-(3x-2)^{2}}}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538953 1072 #uuid "46c80522-dca7-526c-a225-6cc3d9755240" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538953 1074 "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{(6x-4)\\sqrt{9-(3x-2)^{2}}}=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{6(x-\\frac{2}{3})\\sqrt{9-9{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}}}=\\frac{1}{18}\\int \\frac{1}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})\\sqrt{1-{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=x-\\frac{2}{3}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18}\\int \\frac{1}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})\\sqrt{1-{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{18}\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(x-\\frac{2}{3})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\frac{3x-2}{3})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538954 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538808 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538954 1069 "q-107262" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538954 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538954 1071 "![](images/df/df0c2e8a1af51ce0765ca2689f2ee1043d940d2abe2f8f18616786ace1fb18ac.png)\n\nThe volume of the cube above is $8\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}.$ What is the length of its sides?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538954 1072 #uuid "4a3d3c61-1d56-511a-8971-a92e96959ca8" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538954 1074 "Since the volume of a cube is \n\n$$\nV={s}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the length of a side, we can find $s$ as follows:\n\n$$\ns=\\sqrt[3]{V}=\\sqrt[3]{8}=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of a side is $2\\,\\text{ft}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538955 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538920 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538955 1069 "q-113798" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538955 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538955 1071 "$\\frac{2}{5u}÷\\frac{3}{7v}=$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538955 1072 #uuid "4dcf3cdc-27a5-54d1-8c1d-7131e7d4a1a0" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538955 1074 "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{5u}÷\\frac{3}{7v},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5u}÷\\frac{3}{7v} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{5u}\\cdot \\frac{7v}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{14v}{15u} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538956 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538903 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538956 1069 "q-100710" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538956 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538956 1071 "If $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-52)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12),$ then what is the value of $x?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538956 1072 #uuid "5004d37a-89b2-53f7-9b0b-a8e11a406464" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538956 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-52) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12) \\\\ {x}^{2}-52 & =12 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =64 \\\\ x & =\\pm 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=8$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-52) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({8}^{2}-52) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(64-52) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=8$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-8$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-52) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((-8)^{2}-52) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(64-52) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-8$ is a valid solution.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"$x=\\pm 8.$\"" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538957 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538837 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538957 1069 "q-81875" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538957 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538957 1071 "![](images/69/692f2ca65354bd1563ef8a08658644c2cb1793e7c1dfa5e415b63ec95e91b114.png)\n\nWhat is the volume of the cube?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538957 1072 #uuid "5139923c-05c3-5b4c-afc7-050e8c844fa1" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538957 1074 "The volume $V$ of a cube can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nV={s}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the side length of the cube.\n\nSubstituting $s=5\\,\\text{m}$ into the formula, we get \n\n$$\nV=(5\\,\\text{m})^{3}=125\\,{\\text{ m}}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538827 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538958 1069 "q-218724" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538958 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538958 1071 "The dimensions of a package in the shape of a rectangular solid are $0.9\\,\\text{m}\\times 0.9\\,\\text{m}\\times 1.2\\,\\text{m},$ where each dimension was measured to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{m}.$ Rounded to the nearest tenth of a cubic meter, what is the upper bound for the volume of the package?\n\nTo the nearest tenth of a cubic meter, the upper bound for the volume of the package is\n{{field-1}}${\\text{m}}^{3}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538958 1072 #uuid "559e7325-1f2d-5d6d-99bc-9f9ffa4b9d3b" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538958 1074 "Each dimension is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{m}.$ So, the greatest possible error of these measurements is\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.1\\,\\text{m}}{2}=0.05\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the *upper bound* for the volume of the package. Therefore, we require the *upper bound* of each dimension.\n\nThe upper bounds for each dimension are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.9\\,\\text{m}+0.05\\,\\text{m}=0.95\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & 0.9\\,\\text{m}+0.05\\,\\text{m}=0.95\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & 1.2\\,\\text{m}+0.05\\,\\text{m}=1.25\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the upper bound for the volume of the package is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =0.95\\,\\text{m}\\times 0.95\\,\\text{m}\\times 1.25\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =1.128\\,125\\,{\\text{m}}^{3} \\\\  & ≈1.1\\,{\\text{m}}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth of a cubic meter." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538870 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538959 1069 "q-83971" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538959 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538959 1071 "The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$  infected with a certain virus  follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=2P-0.000\\,01{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in days, since the virus started spreading. If only one person was infected initially, how many people will have been infected after $5$ days?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538959 1072 #uuid "59e76c83-715a-52bc-a7bd-8ad37145a622" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538959 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =2P-0.000\\,01{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.000\\,01P(200\\,000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,01,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=200\\,000.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{200\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(200\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,01)t}}=\\frac{200\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-2t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=1$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{200\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-2(0)}} & =1 \\\\ 1+C & =200\\,000 \\\\ C & =199\\,999\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{200\\,000}{1+199\\,999{e}^{-2t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=5$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5) & =\\frac{200\\,000}{1+199\\,999{e}^{-2\\cdot 5}} \\\\  & ≈19\\,841.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $5$ days, approximately $19\\,841$ people have been infected." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538813 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538820 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538839 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538856 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538960 1069 "q-269771" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538960 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538960 1071 "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$38$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$34$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538960 1072 #uuid "5cda63cf-1401-57bf-8fcd-cd3ff96bbaac" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538960 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2{y}_{1} & 2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 2+1 \\\\ 3+2\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 14 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{3}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & {x}_{1} & 1 \\\\ 1 & {x}_{3} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 14 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 38 & 52 & 34\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538873 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538961 1069 "q-103124" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538961 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538961 1071 "A company initially had $40\\,320$ followers on a social media platform. If the number of followers declines by $\\frac{1}{8}$ every month after some bad publicity, which expression gives approximately the number of followers after $4$ months?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538961 1072 #uuid "5e4151ac-1192-5c41-bdd3-5c80d61ad30d" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538961 1074 "If the number of followers reduces by $\\frac{1}{8}$ every month, then $\\frac{7}{8}$ of the followers remains every month.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ month, there will be $40\\,320\\cdot (\\frac{7}{8})$ followers.\n\n- After $2$ months, there will be $40\\,320\\cdot {(\\frac{7}{8})}^{2}$ followers.\n\n- After $3$ months, there will be approximately $40\\,320\\cdot {(\\frac{7}{8})}^{3}$ followers.\n\n- After $4$ months, there will be approximately $40\\,320\\cdot {(\\frac{7}{8})}^{4}$ followers." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538962 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538815 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538962 1069 "q-117725" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538962 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538962 1071 "![](images/a6/a6a3a00964fb1cc8d9139ea29ea2f1a8dc8c0dc52747c4808b69ccb18765ae39.png)\n\nHow many faces does the octagonal pyramid shown above have?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538962 1072 #uuid "626be19c-429d-568e-bc0b-7f6bea746a2b" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538962 1074 "The octagonal pyramid has $8$ triangular faces and $1$ octagonal face, so it has $9$ faces in total." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538963 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538918 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538963 1069 "q-136955" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538963 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538963 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ given that the continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x+2}{8},\\,-2\\le x\\le 2.\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538963 1072 #uuid "629a07aa-1bdb-505b-9cdf-8118c6579d3f" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538963 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ and a function $r(x),$ the rule of the lazy statistician states that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X)]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nr(x)={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{8}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{32}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{12}]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{16}{32}+\\frac{8}{12}]-[\\frac{16}{32}-\\frac{8}{12}] \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538849 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538964 1069 "q-118657" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538964 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538964 1071 "Each side of a cubic room has length $\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{yd}.$ What is the volume of the room?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538964 1072 #uuid "62d14dcd-2286-5ac0-9da8-8fd4144e472b" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538964 1074 "We need to find the volume of a cube.\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cube can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nV={s}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the side length of the cube.\n\nSubstituting $s=\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{yd}$ into the formula, we get \n\n$$\nV={(\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{yd})}^{3}=\\frac{343}{8}\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the room is $\\frac{343}{8}\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538965 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538807 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538965 1069 "q-30011" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538965 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538965 1071 "Given that $\\frac{{x}^{2}-64}{x-5}=a$ and $\\frac{(x-8)^{2}}{x-5}=b,$ what is $a÷b?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538965 1072 #uuid "64ed62a5-f26a-57d4-b199-8c8c51b737ac" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538965 1074 "To compute\n\n$$\na÷b=\\frac{{x}^{2}-64}{x-5}÷\\frac{(x-8)^{2}}{x-5},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-64}{x-5}÷\\frac{(x-8)^{2}}{x-5} & = \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}-64}{x-5}\\cdot \\frac{x-5}{(x-8)^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{(x-8)(x+8)}{x-5}\\cdot \\frac{x-5}{(x-8)(x-8)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{(x-8)}(x+8)}{\\cancel{x-5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{x-5}}{\\cancel{(x-8)}(x-8)} & = \\\\ (x+8)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x-8)} & = \\\\ \\frac{x+8}{x-8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538966 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538812 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538966 1069 "q-68483" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538966 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538966 1071 "Sophia and Charlie decide to go to the sea to study some marine species. While Charlie dives $14.2$ meters below sea level, Sophia waits for him in the boat, $2.5$ meters above sea level. How many meters above Charlie is Sophia?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538966 1072 #uuid "65afe218-5b40-51ad-8f32-78468d52b1c6" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538966 1074 "To determine the distance between Charlie and Sophia, we must calculate the difference between Sophia's location and Charlie's location. This gives\n\n$$\n2.5-(-14.2)=2.5+14.2=16.7\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nThus, Sophia is located $16.7\\,\\text{m}$ above Charlie." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538967 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538811 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538967 1069 "q-269555" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538967 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538967 1071 "Consider the vector-valued function $f:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x},$ the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538967 1072 #uuid "67046761-6838-5e5c-bd1f-8e1ae02d3dbf" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538967 1074 "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{m\\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(x)$ where $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{3}$, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, the total derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{3}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{2}+{x}_{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the total derivative is a constant matrix, evaluating it at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ gives the same matrix." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538845 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538968 1069 "q-97778" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538968 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538968 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{(3x-15)\\sqrt{10x-{x}^{2}-24}}\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538968 1072 #uuid "6858ad14-b7bc-531f-aa86-afb1603436a5" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538968 1074 "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+10x-24 & =-({x}^{2}-10x+24) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2}+24) \\\\  & =-((x-5)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(x-5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{(3x-15)\\sqrt{10x-{x}^{2}-24}}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{(x-5)\\sqrt{1-(x-5)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=x-5.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(3x-15)\\sqrt{10x-{x}^{2}-24}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{(x-5)\\sqrt{1-(x-5)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(x-5)+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538969 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538911 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538969 1069 "q-103238" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538969 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538969 1071 "Evaluate $|x-5|-|3-x|+2$ for $x=8.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538969 1072 #uuid "68630caf-af8f-50ee-bc03-18c9657f8cb4" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538969 1074 "We substitute $x=8$ and then evaluate the absolute values before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-5|-|3-x|+2 & = \\\\ |8-5|-|3-8|+2 & = \\\\ |3|-|-5|+2 & = \\\\ 3-5+2 & = \\\\ 0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538828 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538970 1069 "q-97901" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538970 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538970 1071 "$\\int -\\frac{6}{|x+3|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x+13}}\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538970 1072 #uuid "69f3a20c-d1a0-5eb2-90e0-cb5356408eda" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538970 1074 "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x+13 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}+13 \\\\  & =(x+3)^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{6}{|x+3|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x+13}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{6}{|x+3|\\sqrt{4+(x+3)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{6}{2|\\frac{x+3}{2}|\\sqrt{4+4{(\\frac{x+3}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{|\\frac{x+3}{2}|\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{x+3}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{x+3}{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{|\\frac{x+3}{2}|\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{x+3}{2})}^{2}}}\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡u+C \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538971 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538912 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538971 1069 "q-215803" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538971 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538971 1071 "If $y=0,$ then $|2y+3{|}^{3}-|y+2|=${{field-1}}." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538971 1072 #uuid "6a42fde9-ed9b-5e9d-86c6-940fc99b7d25" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538971 1074 "We substitute $y=0$ and then evaluate the absolute values before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2y+3{|}^{3}-|y+2| & = \\\\ |2(0)+3{|}^{3}-|0+2| & = \\\\ |0+3{|}^{3}-|0+2| & = \\\\ |3{|}^{3}-|2| & = \\\\ {3}^{3}-2 & = \\\\ 27-2 & = \\\\ 25 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538972 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538909 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538972 1069 "q-350681" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538972 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538972 1071 "What is the sum of the series $\\,1-\\frac{1}{9\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{1}{81\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{3}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯\\,?$\n\nThe sum of the series is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538972 1072 #uuid "6b7d68eb-3099-57db-9579-cc9c26274e37" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538972 1074 "We recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos\\,(\\frac{1}{3}) & =1-\\frac{{(1/3)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(1/3)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{(1/3)}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ cos\\,(\\frac{1}{3}) & =1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{4}\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{3}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ cos\\,(\\frac{1}{3}) & =1-\\frac{1}{9\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{1}{81\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{3}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $cos\\,(\\frac{1}{3}).$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538840 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538973 1069 "q-100380" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538973 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538973 1071 "${({5}^{2})}^{8}=$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538973 1072 #uuid "6ff4ab80-f137-5311-afce-bd7745588b1f" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538973 1074 "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{({5}^{2})}^{8}={5}^{2\\cdot 8}={5}^{16}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538974 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538859 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538974 1069 "q-117729" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538974 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538974 1071 "![](images/43/4342172bb8ccb125f66b33b3ed034cfc2e3bf62a1ddd2cd35449cabf37fdca02.png)\n\nHow many vertices does the rectangular pyramid shown above have?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538974 1072 #uuid "70d03337-b672-59ea-90e6-81097f2438d1" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538974 1074 "Vertices are points that connect the straight line segments (edges) of a polyhedron.\n\nThe rectangular pyramid has $5$ vertices in total, as shown below. \n\n![](images/e5/e5b1567b12493d6e756c93472a535d631cd21ba3281685b38ad990add9d755bc.png)" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538975 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538844 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538975 1069 "q-97766" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538975 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538975 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)\\sqrt{-4{x}^{2}-8x}}\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538975 1072 #uuid "726516a6-793d-5373-8298-c27b8c843f27" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538975 1074 "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{x}^{2}-8x & =-4({x}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =-4({x}^{2}+2\\cdot x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4[(x+1)^{2}-1] \\\\  & =4[1-(x+1)^{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)\\sqrt{-4{x}^{2}-8x}}\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)\\sqrt{4[1-(x+1)^{2}]}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)\\sqrt{1-(x+1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=x+1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)\\sqrt{-4{x}^{2}-8x}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)\\sqrt{1-(x+1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(x+1)+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538833 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538976 1069 "q-346635" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538976 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538976 1071 "![](images/47/479e2f20fea6b5e22b11fa8f8e5015ad81a57a10ed43a53d5669b0363d33b185.png)\n\nA conical grain container has a height of $450\\,\\text{cm}$ and the radius at the bottom of the container is $90\\,\\text{cm}.$ The container is being emptied so that the distance $h$ from the top of the container to the grain surface increases at a rate of $\\frac{25}{3h}\\,\\text{cm/min}.$ Find the rate at which the volume of the grain is decreasing when the grain level is $180\\,\\text{cm}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe volume of grain is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538976 1072 #uuid "75da158e-460b-5ea3-876d-2ea3f7a43576" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538976 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=450\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the conical container, and $R=90\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the bottom. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the grain level. We call $h$ the distance from the top of the container to the surface of the grain, $r$ the radius of the cone at the grain surface, and $v$ the volume of the empty space in the container. All of these quantities change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{25}{3h}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nwhich is positive because the distance from the top of the container to the grain surface increases as the level of the grain in the container decreases. \n\nWe want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when the level of the grain is $180\\,\\text{cm}.$ At this moment, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =H-180 \\\\  & =450-180 \\\\  & =270\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the empty space located between the container's top and the grain surface must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{90}{450} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{5})}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{75}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{75}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{25}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{25}\\pi {h}^{2}(\\frac{25}{3h}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi (270) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =90\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of grain is decreasing at a rate of $90\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538915 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538977 1069 "q-120240" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538977 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538977 1071 "The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ with food poisoning in an small town follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.15P-0.000\\,03{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in weeks, since the first reported cases. If there were $100$ cases initially, how many people with food poisoning there are after $4$ weeks?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538977 1072 #uuid "80763a51-c6ab-5b5d-83cb-358601dd22aa" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538977 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.15P-0.000\\,03{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.000\\,03P(5\\,000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,03,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=5\\,000.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{5\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(5\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,03)t}}=\\frac{5\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.15t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=100$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =100 \\\\ \\frac{5\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.15(0)}} & =100 \\\\ 1+C & =50 \\\\ C & =49\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{5\\,000}{1+49{e}^{-0.15t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=4$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4) & =\\frac{5\\,000}{1+49{e}^{-0.15(4)}} \\\\  & ≈179.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $4$ weeks, there will be approximately $179$ people with food poisoning." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538978 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538871 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538978 1069 "q-107255" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538978 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538978 1071 "![](images/a8/a8d41cd80424e11002d30b26ee1eec86e17f343a8bb9765013fced59e3e2885b.png)\n\nWhat is the volume of the cube?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538978 1072 #uuid "81d575e3-b933-5487-8eb2-7aae2b3ca41b" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538978 1074 "The volume $V$ of a cube can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nV={s}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the side length of the cube.\n\nSubstituting $s=4\\,\\text{in}$ into the formula, we get \n\n$$\nV=(4\\,\\text{cm})^{3}=64{\\text{ in}}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538979 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538885 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538979 1069 "q-61871" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538979 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538979 1071 "Elena has $400$ TVs in her store. She expects that the number of TVs will decrease by $2%$ daily due to sales. Approximately how many TVs will be in her store after $3$ days?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538979 1072 #uuid "85541785-a1f8-5b45-843e-637ba62e4e83" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538979 1074 "The number $2%$ (expressed as a decimal) is equal to $0.02.$ Therefore, a decrease of $0.02%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of:\n\n$$\n1-0.02=0.98\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ day, there will be $400\\cdot (0.98)$ TVs remaining.\n\n- After $2$ days, there will be approximately $400\\cdot (0.98)^{2}$ TVs remaining.\n\n- After $3$ days, there will be approximately $400\\cdot (0.98)^{3}$ TVs remaining." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538875 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538980 1069 "q-97781" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538980 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538980 1071 "$\\int \\frac{3}{|3x+1|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}+6x+10}}\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538980 1072 #uuid "87c6c320-8ad0-5a7a-8ea7-78e763a7875c" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538980 1074 "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+6x+10 & =9({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{10}{9}) \\\\  & =9({x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}x+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{10}{9}) \\\\  & =9[{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{|3x+1|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}+6x+10}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{|x+\\frac{1}{3}|\\sqrt{9[1+{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}]}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{|x+\\frac{1}{3}|\\sqrt{1+{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=x+\\frac{1}{3}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{|x+\\frac{1}{3}|\\sqrt{1+{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{3})+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538832 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538981 1069 "q-353118" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538981 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538981 1071 "Find the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of the geometric series \n\n$$\n-10-2-\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{25}-⋯.\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\infty }=$  \n{{field-1}}" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538981 1072 #uuid "8ad57966-24c0-50f4-b632-7291950e6a6e" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538981 1074 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term ${a}_{1}=-10$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(-2)}{(-10)}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10}{(1-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{(\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538982 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538854 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538982 1069 "q-98005" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538982 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538982 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{12+4x-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538982 1072 #uuid "8dc0f52e-b98b-54ec-bcf4-1fa4702113db" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538982 1074 "First, we complete the square for the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12+4x-{x}^{2} & =-({x}^{2}-4x-12) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}-12) \\\\  & =-((x-2)^{2}-16) \\\\  & =16-(x-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator of our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{12+4x-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{16-(x-2)^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{16}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{1}{4}(x-2))}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{1}{4}(x-2).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{1}{4}(x-2))}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{16}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}(x-2))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-2}{4})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{x-2}{4}|>1.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538983 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538855 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538983 1069 "q-37402" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538983 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538983 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538983 1072 #uuid "9820b99c-2acb-5f80-b231-9670a302c372" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538983 1074 "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(2)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ -(\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{8}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{1}=-1.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538893 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538984 1069 "q-1131" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538984 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538984 1071 "Solve the equation ${log}_{12}⁡({v}^{2}+35)={log}_{12}⁡(-12v-1).$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538984 1072 #uuid "98df2a56-4669-5921-84a6-4e54451b6791" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538984 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{12}⁡({v}^{2}+35) & ={log}_{12}⁡(-12v-1) \\\\ {v}^{2}+35 & =-12v-1 \\\\ {v}^{2}+12v+36 & =0 \\\\ (v+6)^{2} & =0 \\\\ v & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $v=-6$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{12}⁡({v}^{2}+35) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{12}⁡(-12v-1) \\\\ {log}_{12}⁡((-6)^{2}+35) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{12}⁡(-12(-6)-1) \\\\ {log}_{12}⁡(71) & ={log}_{12}⁡(71).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v=-6$ is a valid solution." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538910 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538985 1069 "q-79867" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538985 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538985 1071 "Solve the equation ${log}_{3}⁡(x+4)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(25).$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538985 1072 #uuid "9b0b21ed-9399-541e-870b-dc048c3a5954" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538985 1074 "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(25) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{3}⁡({25}^{1/2}) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{3}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{3}⁡(5) \\\\ x+4 & =5 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{3}⁡(1+4) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(25).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a valid solution." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538986 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538900 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538986 1069 "q-103098" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538986 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538986 1071 "What is the sum of the series $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{(5\\pi /6)^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{(5\\pi /6)^{5}}{5!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}(5\\pi /6)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\,?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538986 1072 #uuid "9b70f694-75c4-56a5-8060-3f93ae37aa40" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538986 1074 "We recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{(5\\pi /6)^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{(5\\pi /6)^{5}}{5!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}(5\\pi /6)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯ \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{(5\\pi /6)^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{(5\\pi /6)^{5}}{5!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}(5\\pi /6)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538987 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538919 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538987 1069 "q-121589" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538987 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538987 1071 "![](images/ad/ad22dcd0793dbd4f7135598f94d3cfce514b8966bb18ea7edbe34058c208404e.png)\n\nA child is tearing the border of a paper party cone-shaped hat in such a way that the volume inside the hat decreases at $10\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$ The height of the cone is always two times its base radius. At what rate is the radius of the hat decreasing when the radius is $15\\,\\text{in}?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538987 1072 #uuid "9d29b6dd-70c7-586b-8075-827c46bfd599" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538987 1074 "Let's call $h$ the height of the hat and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-10\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=15\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height is always two times its base diameter. So, we have $h=2r.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}(2r) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =2\\pi (15)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -10 & =450\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{45\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the hat is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{45\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538988 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538880 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538988 1069 "q-118200" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538988 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538988 1071 "![](images/56/5625952a4bc43f7c1a8c7ecc73b48c04da706d3322e69a4648c07e602e5803e9.png)\n\nHow many edges does the octagonal prism shown above have?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538988 1072 #uuid "a2533114-7be2-5ce8-9f95-31aefb736c4b" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538988 1074 "Edges are straight line segments that connect the vertices of a polyhedron.\n\nThe octagonal prism has $24$ edges in total, as shown below.    \n\n![](images/97/977252469ea701078eb86353643a7978561b21be17154e72848581bb4453b223.png)" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538989 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538891 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538989 1069 "q-31996" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538989 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538989 1071 "![](images/13/135ce8e277c0895d36972e12fb7df68447af79765c4acaaba6fb0e63fc498d73.png)\n\nThe height of an ice-cream cone is $6\\,\\text{cm}$ and the radius at the top of the cone is $1.5\\,\\text{cm}.$ Some ice-cream is leaking from a small hole at the bottom at a rate of $\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.$ If the ice-cream level is $4\\,\\text{cm}$ at a particular moment, how fast is the ice-cream level decreasing at that moment?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538989 1072 #uuid "a327d128-352b-50f9-99e7-ff67c5f44d4a" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538989 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the cone. Let's call $H=6\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the cone, and $R=1.5\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the ice cream level. We call $h$ the height of the ice cream in the cone and $r$ the radius of the ice cream top surface. Both of these quantities, and the ice cream volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find the rate of change of the ice cream level $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=4\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of the ice cream:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cone and the ice cream part of the cone must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{1.5}{6} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{4})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\pi {h}^{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{48}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{16}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of the ice cream level in the cone when $h=4\\,\\text{cm}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\pi  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\pi ({4}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-1\\,\\text{cm/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the ice cream is decreasing at a rate of $1\\,\\text{cm/min}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538865 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538990 1069 "q-359420" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538990 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538990 1071 "![](images/ff/ffdfbbfcbc2a01e79fd9490f05744b2d0bf1d24dd616a0b318ee95acfa26e9ba.png)\n\nA cone-shaped pile of salt is being depleted so that the volume of the pile decreases at $5\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{hr}.$ The height of the pile is always one-half of its base diameter. Find the rate of the pile’s height decrease when the height is $5\\,\\text{m}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe height is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{m}/\\text{hr}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538990 1072 #uuid "a403c1d3-ab4a-51b5-846b-0a043ce7d6fd" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538990 1074 "Let's call $h$ the height of the pile and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-5\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{hr},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=5\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the height is always one-half of its base diameter. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{1}{2}d \\\\ h & =\\frac{1}{2}(2r) \\\\ r & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi (h)^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\pi  & =\\pi (5)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -5\\pi  & =25\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{hr}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the pile is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{hr}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538892 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538991 1069 "q-117728" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538991 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538991 1071 "![](images/ee/ee75ad73a353e3ddea1e2f43fa99ae4e28f3153ca44bf21d13dce8fe29e9c223.png)\n\nHow many vertices does the rectangular prism shown above have?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538991 1072 #uuid "a7669acf-31b3-5eb2-8d89-5b43d2dbc721" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538991 1074 "Vertices are points that connect the straight line segments (edges) of a polyhedron. \n\nThe rectangular prism has $8$ vertices in total, as shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2e85a2e039a8e854cefc508e9cb4bee3d2b1fa4a3ba2af3fed99e998e47189b5.png)" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538992 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538884 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538992 1069 "q-160305" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538992 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538992 1071 "What is the distance between the points $(1,-3)$  and $(5,-3)?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538992 1072 #uuid "a94dc93f-684c-5299-b244-16888ab7a25a" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538992 1074 "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/b3/b3a34b10cf329cea0e80959562484c07316a582d55395f8bf7aa951d2aca8567.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $4$ units, so the distance equals $4.$\n\n![](images/15/15afca52169a74eb433654bc992cc3d19e191a92704d7d845d0c49071800052e.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/72/7298280773fb08b221742571e76ba5f422a77a2e70abd4b94085d602a196e8a7.png)\n\nThe point $(1,-3)$ is at a distance of $1$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $(5,-3)$ is at a distance of $5$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $5-1=4.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538883 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538993 1069 "q-353252" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538993 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538993 1071 "The growth rate of the rabbit population $P(t)$ in a wildlife preserve is jointly proportional to the rabbit population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the preserve’s carrying capacity and the rabbit population.\n\nGiven that the constant of proportionality is $0.0025$ and the carrying capacity is $1200,$ find an expression for the rabbit population after $t$ years, given that there were $200$ rabbits in the preserve initially.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"right\" rowspan=\"2\">\n      $P(t)\\,=\\,\\,$\n    </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n      $1200$\n    </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\">\n      $1\\,+\\,$ {{field-1}}\n    </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538993 1072 #uuid "aa31a49a-8ce4-5ed7-acd6-a1443ddcc296" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538993 1074 "Since the rabbit population’s growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the rabbit population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the rabbit population, the rabbit population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.0025,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=1200.$ Therefore, the rabbit population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.0025\\,P(1200-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1200}{1+C{e}^{-(1200\\cdot 0.0025)t}}=\\frac{1200}{1+C{e}^{-3t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=200$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1200}{1+C{e}^{-3\\cdot 0}} & =200 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{1200}{200} \\\\ C & =6-1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1200}{1+5{e}^{-3t}}.\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538994 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538838 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538994 1069 "q-147015" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538994 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538994 1071 "On a field trip to a theme park, $150$ students out of $500$ rode a roller coaster. What percentage of students rode a roller coaster?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538994 1072 #uuid "aa408be7-da1f-5fc4-a78a-e723f9d79c8a" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538994 1074 "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of students who rode a roller coaster, which is $150,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of students, which is $500.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=150÷500=\\frac{150}{500}.\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{150}{500}=\\frac{150÷5}{500÷5}=\\frac{30}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{30}{100}=30%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $30%$ of the students rode a roller coaster." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538809 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538995 1069 "q-61573" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538995 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538995 1071 "![](images/64/64b8ccd3dbdf89ceb96027f9bcc4abbfe951447ea0597b518fa179cd2ec15687.png)\n\nWhich of the following is a polyhedron with exactly $7$ faces?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538995 1072 #uuid "abca4998-637c-5086-844b-6b4ef93fbdda" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538995 1074 "Let's examine the solids one-by-one.\n\n- I is a polyhedron but it has only $5$ faces.\n\n- II is not a polyhedron (its top and bottom faces are not polygons).\n\n- III is a polyhedron and it has exactly $7$ faces." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538850 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538996 1069 "q-118056" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538996 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538996 1071 "![](images/29/29bb075857ef911549b1f1ec3ea2184c08da26ee2c61bc241748d7fd85ddc0d6.png)\n\nHow many vertices does the octagonal prism shown above have?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538996 1072 #uuid "abf367e7-fbd7-5dc5-a345-a46158845395" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538996 1074 "The vertices of a polyhedron are the corners where edges meet.\n\nThe octagonal prism has $16$ vertices in total, as shown below. \n\n![](images/28/2864c05c428fe3d8c9fa503d0ede7bab2ab75409cf7c2b8ac3357564c36d15db.png)" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538997 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538835 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538997 1069 "q-353145" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538997 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538997 1071 "Find the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of the geometric series \n\n$$\n2.5+1.5+0.9+0.54+⋯.\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\infty }=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538997 1072 #uuid "b0043402-5216-56a5-82e2-182b3eaa6817" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538997 1074 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=2.5$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{1.5}{2.5}=0.6.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.5}{1-0.6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.5}{0.4} \\\\  & =6.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538901 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538998 1069 "q-103117" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538998 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538998 1071 "What is the sum of the series $\\,1-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{64\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{4}}{4\\,096\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{\\pi }^{2n}}{{8}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯\\,?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538998 1072 #uuid "b2664ee6-8acb-52ad-889d-ed63a283e637" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538998 1074 "We recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯-\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =1-\\frac{{(\\pi /8)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(\\pi /8)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{(\\pi /8)}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =1-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{{8}^{2}\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{4}}{{8}^{4}\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{\\pi }^{2n}}{{8}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =1-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{64\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{4}}{4\\,096\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{\\pi }^{2n}}{{8}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}).$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538826 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538999 1069 "q-353150" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538999 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538999 1071 "Find the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of the geometric series \n\n$$\n9.6-0.96+0.096-0.0096+⋯.\n$$\n\n Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\infty }=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538999 1072 #uuid "b2fe4d85-a3ec-5968-ad10-7f316b6ab6b7" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538999 1074 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=9.6$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{-0.96}{9.6}=-0.1.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{9.6}{1-(-0.1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9.6}{1+0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9.6}{1.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{960}{110} \\\\  & =\\frac{96}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539000 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538848 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539000 1069 "q-27729" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539000 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539000 1071 "Solve the equation ${log}_{8}⁡({x}^{2}+19x)={log}_{8}⁡(10x-8).$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539000 1072 #uuid "b409a03d-9253-56f1-9515-c6248febada5" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539000 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡({x}^{2}+19x) & ={log}_{8}⁡(10x-8) \\\\ {x}^{2}+19x & =10x-8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9x+8 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x+8) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡({x}^{2}+19x) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(10x-8) \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡((-1)^{2}+19(-1)) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(10(-1)-8) \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(-18) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(-18)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not valid because ${log}_{8}⁡(-18)$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=-1$ is not a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-8$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡({x}^{2}+19x) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(10x-8) \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡((-8)^{2}+19(-8)) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(10(-8)-8) \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(-88) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(-88)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not valid because ${log}_{8}⁡(-88)$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=-8$ is not a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"No solutions.\"" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539001 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538836 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539001 1069 "q-212313" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539001 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539001 1071 "If $x=1,$ then $3-|{2}^{-x}-\\frac{5}{2}|=${{field-1}}." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539001 1072 #uuid "b8991b67-f1ef-53f9-975f-0551777dc4d1" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539001 1074 "We substitute $x=1$ and then evaluate the absolute value before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-|{2}^{-x}-\\frac{5}{2}| & = \\\\ 3-|{2}^{-1}-\\frac{5}{2}| & = \\\\ 3-|\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}| & = \\\\ 3-|-2| & = \\\\ 3-2 & = \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539002 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538819 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539002 1069 "q-50942" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539002 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539002 1071 "$\\,1-\\frac{(\\pi /2)^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{(\\pi /2)^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{(\\pi /2)}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯=$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539002 1072 #uuid "b939d5df-d459-5fd4-9292-d3dcc1c6afac" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539002 1074 "We recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =1-\\frac{{(\\pi /2)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(\\pi /2)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{(\\pi /2)}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ 0 & =1-\\frac{(\\pi /2)^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{(\\pi /2)^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{(\\pi /2)}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $0.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539003 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538868 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539003 1069 "q-61783" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539003 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539003 1071 "![](images/77/77f8b3cc305a0d7a05b9b73d2a0eacdf92046945d1c7cced10d83d35c6a1aef1.png)\n\nHow many vertices does the cube shown above have?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539003 1072 #uuid "b9e5b4b8-1fed-5910-8dea-7343ab88cff4" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539003 1074 "Vertices are points that connect the straight line segments (edges) of a polyhedron. \n\nThe cube has $8$ vertices in total, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b9/b9f556dfaef103dadd40a788a600db8e7a9e3a0af8943cf8ce523133bfd16994.png)" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539004 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538816 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539004 1069 "q-117726" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539004 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539004 1071 "![](images/a0/a0cc9c3f9dbb7e7809e6397601311d477fa9a5ddd82aa4600fee01019acc3eaf.png)\n\nWhich of the following is a polyhedron with exactly $5$ faces?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539004 1072 #uuid "c2b71007-470f-55b4-a0ed-e3fb0749c4cb" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539004 1074 "Let's examine the solids one-by-one.\n\n- I is not a polyhedron (its bottom face is not a polygon).\n\n- II  is a polyhedron and it has exactly $5$ faces.\n\n- III is a polyhedron but it has $7$ faces." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539005 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538847 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539005 1069 "q-68615" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539005 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539005 1071 "${(\\frac{4}{7}\\times \\frac{1}{5})}^{5}=$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539005 1072 #uuid "c32986e7-9010-5588-b701-72ad7388c062" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539005 1074 "To raise the product of two numbers to the power of $5$, we can raise each number to the power of $5,$ and then multiply:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{4}{7}\\times \\frac{1}{5})}^{4}={(\\frac{4}{7})}^{5}\\times {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{5}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539006 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538831 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539006 1069 "q-353266" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539006 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539006 1071 "The growth rate of the number of households $P(t)$ participating in a neighborhood composting program is jointly proportional to the number of participating households at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the program’s carrying capacity and the number of participating households. The constant of proportionality is $0.0005$ and the carrying capacity is $800.$\n\nHow many households will be participating after $7$ years given that there were $100$ households participating initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAfter $7$ years, there will be approximately \n{{field-1}} households participating in the program." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539006 1072 #uuid "c384ade7-8329-5b20-a7ce-55cc1c6684d1" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539006 1074 "Since the number of households participating grows at a rate that is *jointly* proportional to the number of households participating at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the number of households participating, the number of households participating follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.0005,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=800.$ Therefore, the growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.0005\\,P(800-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-(800\\cdot 0.0005)t}}=\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-0.4t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=100$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-0.4(0)}} & =100 \\\\ 1+C & =8 \\\\ C & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{800}{1+7{e}^{-0.4t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=7$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(7) & =\\frac{800}{1+7{e}^{-0.4(7)}} \\\\  & ≈561.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $7$ years, there will be approximately $561$ households participating in the program." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538878 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539007 1069 "q-83287" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539007 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539007 1071 "What is the sum of the series $-3+\\frac{27}{3!}-\\frac{243}{5!}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}(-3)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\,?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539007 1072 #uuid "c45730a9-37f0-5369-8e28-cf166dacf491" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539007 1074 "We recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-3$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3) & =(-3)-\\frac{(-3)^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{(-3)^{5}}{5!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}(-3)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯ \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3 & =-3+\\frac{27}{3!}-\\frac{243}{5!}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}(-3)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539008 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538864 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539008 1069 "q-107265" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539008 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539008 1071 "![](images/ce/ceaf4ee652448eecc115fbe7f53a3ad2ac9bf255ece8c04674fe2d8d7d6fb324.png)\n\nThe volume of the cube above is $512\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.$ What is the length of its sides?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539008 1072 #uuid "c484b581-e8de-5427-867e-86ac0d8ace75" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539008 1074 "Since the volume of a cube is \n\n$$\nV={s}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the length of a side, we can find $s$ as follows:\n\n$$\ns=\\sqrt[3]{V}=\\sqrt[3]{512}=8\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of a side is $8\\,\\text{in}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539009 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538890 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539009 1069 "q-100713" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539009 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539009 1071 "If $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-56)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(65),$ then what is the value of $x?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539009 1072 #uuid "c6d686ab-e99a-50e7-9f05-fa963ea40c5a" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539009 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-56) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(65) \\\\ {x}^{2}-56 & =65 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =121 \\\\ x & =\\pm 11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=11$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-56) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({11}^{2}-56) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(121-56) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(65).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=11$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-11$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-56) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((-11)^{2}-56) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(121-56) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(65).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-11$ is a valid solution.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"$x=\\pm 11.$\"" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538857 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539010 1069 "q-118058" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539010 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539010 1071 "![](images/53/5340c620eb36d20e94b0f29b249d86906314de502a59cb303436013a02502c04.png)\n\nHow many edges does the rectangular pyramid shown above have?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539010 1072 #uuid "cac1350f-3928-55d2-9cd4-d37e44265e2e" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539010 1074 "Edges are straight line segments that connect the vertices of a polyhedron. \n\nThe rectangular pyramid has $8$ edges in total, as shown below.\n\n![](images/df/df95bc61487094c17be2bd9ba267aa85c33d26bed1a74726d9e8d32f546a245b.png)" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539011 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538823 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539011 1069 "q-46386" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539011 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539011 1071 "The dimensions of a cubic water tank are $2.4\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.4\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.4\\,\\text{m},$  where each dimension is measured to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{m}.$ Rounded to the nearest tenth of a cubic meter, what is the smallest possible volume of the tank?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539011 1072 #uuid "cc1f868e-acd2-53b5-a61f-6b065116ee9a" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539011 1074 "Each dimension is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{m}.$ So, the greatest possible error of these measurements is\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.1\\,\\text{m}}{2}=0.05\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the *smallest* possible volume of the tank. Therefore, we require the *lower bound* of each dimension.\n\nThe lower bounds for each dimension are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2.4\\,\\text{m}-0.05\\,\\text{m}=2.35\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & 2.4\\,\\text{m}-0.05\\,\\text{m}=2.35\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & 2.4\\,\\text{m}-0.05\\,\\text{m}=2.35\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible volume of the tank is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2.35\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.35\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.35\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =12.977\\,875\\,{\\text{m}}^{3} \\\\  & ≈13.0\\,{\\text{m}}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth of a cubic meter." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539012 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538895 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539012 1069 "q-152350" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539012 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539012 1071 "Evaluate $\\frac{\\sqrt{20}}{\\sqrt{5}}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539012 1072 #uuid "d1265e4c-79e5-5207-9e53-54ea1660d1ab" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539012 1074 "A quotient of square roots of two positive numbers is equal to the square root of the quotient of the numbers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{20}}{\\sqrt{5}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{20}{5}}=\\sqrt{4}=2\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539013 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538817 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539013 1069 "q-118216" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539013 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539013 1071 "![](images/b6/b6724cc4db4e8a1b9ea78ea0b60f4d32caba407f2bde3054cd8774d220585ce9.png)\n\nHow many edges does the heptagonal prism shown above have?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539013 1072 #uuid "d54bd97f-cf95-5470-8ffe-f832c2d314c2" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539013 1074 "Edges are straight line segments that connect the vertices of a polyhedron.\n\nThe heptagonal prism has $21$ edges in total, as shown below.  \n\n![](images/ff/ff605f63d6af63d1be4cf3a7423de050bd3d839367b01ca3fbd90a363ebe92f6.png)" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539014 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538867 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539014 1069 "q-61785" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539014 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539014 1071 "![](images/b3/b3e0ebf3efa244f58690a65c7286c30cbefbfcc18fcf10ed757f905e2e13a00c.png)\n\nHow many edges does the hexagonal pyramid shown above have?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539014 1072 #uuid "d9c569b0-a8da-5864-ab52-f0d29ab16b9d" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539014 1074 "Edges are straight line segments that connect the vertices of a polyhedron.\n\nThe hexagonal pyramid has $12$ edges in total, as shown below.\n\n![](images/12/12b92598097da3800d345e5bf0815e4fcf48fea38a2310f8aeb6afdf9176bb0f.png)" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539015 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538916 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539015 1069 "q-107284" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539015 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539015 1071 "A statue is mounted on a pedestal consisting of a cubic block made of concrete. \nEach side of the cube has length  $5\\,\\text{ft}.$ \nWhat is the volume of the cube?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539015 1072 #uuid "db058a82-1fa2-5274-82e0-1236f8c5f86e" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539015 1074 "We need to find the volume of a cube.\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cube can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nV={s}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the side length of the cube.\n\nSubstituting $s=5\\,\\text{ft}$ into the formula, we get \n\n$$\nV={(5\\,\\text{ft})}^{3}=125\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the pedestal is $125\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539016 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538876 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539016 1069 "q-97758" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539016 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539016 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{4-(x+3)^{2}}\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539016 1072 #uuid "dbcc5371-79bd-5123-b706-5a10821a5d21" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539016 1074 "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{4-(x+3)^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{4-4{(\\frac{x+3}{2})}^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x+3}{2})}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{x+3}{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x+3}{2})}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{x+3}{2}|>1.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539017 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538810 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539017 1069 "q-98008" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539017 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539017 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{12x-{x}^{2}-33}\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539017 1072 #uuid "dc07885c-59ca-5761-a1b0-43a8e8cb6e2b" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539017 1074 "First, we complete the square for the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-{x}^{2}-33 & =-({x}^{2}-12x+33) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}-{6}^{2}+33) \\\\  & =-((x-6)^{2}-3) \\\\  & =3-(x-6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator of our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{12x-{x}^{2}-33}\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{3-(x-6)^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(x-6))}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(x-6).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(x-6))}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(x-6))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-6}{\\sqrt{3}})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{x-6}{\\sqrt{3}}|>1.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539018 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538907 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539018 1069 "q-97762" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539018 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539018 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)\\sqrt{4x-{x}^{2}-3}}\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539018 1072 #uuid "e0181c3f-486e-569d-aaa1-064715b86f7e" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539018 1074 "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-{x}^{2}-3 & =-({x}^{2}-4x+3) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}+3) \\\\  & =-((x-2)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(x-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)\\sqrt{4x-{x}^{2}-3}}\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)\\sqrt{1-(x-2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=x-2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)\\sqrt{4x-{x}^{2}-3}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)\\sqrt{1-(x-2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(x-2)+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539019 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538886 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539019 1069 "q-51559" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539019 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539019 1071 "The $8$th and $9$th terms of an arithmetic sequence are $17$ and $21,$ respectively. Determine the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539019 1072 #uuid "e16d4c55-4032-51af-b361-3c91c642bb36" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539019 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$ \n\nSince the terms ${a}_{8}=17$ and ${a}_{9}=21$ are consecutive, we can subtract them to find the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{9}-{a}_{8} \\\\  & =21-17 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{8}=17,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{8}$ is $7$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{8} & ={a}_{1}+7d \\\\ 17 & ={a}_{1}+7(4) \\\\ 17 & ={a}_{1}+28 \\\\ 17-28 & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-11+(n-1)(4) \\\\  & =-11+4n-4 \\\\  & =4n-15\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539020 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538922 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539020 1069 "q-61808" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539020 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539020 1071 "![](images/8f/8f4dd8fe8c120eabbe5a1ad6413f6f0cd9cd3d22efe8b13e25ac3d2a916f3258.png)\n\nHow many faces does the pentagonal prism shown above have?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539020 1072 #uuid "e7d52a12-a694-5621-a2e6-4ed8ddacf1a9" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539020 1074 "The pentagonal prism has $2$ pentagonal faces and $5$ rectangular faces, so it has $7$ faces in total." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539021 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538905 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539021 1069 "q-107263" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539021 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539021 1071 "![](images/3e/3ebe2e4b3436088aa5385757492a8a1f27add8927ea30de71a32a6dc596083cd.png)\n\nThe volume of the cube above is $1\\,000\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.$ What is the length of its sides?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539021 1072 #uuid "e83a5e5d-4e50-55da-82b2-166ada387fd5" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539021 1074 "Since the volume of a cube is \n\n$$\nV={s}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the length of a side, we can find $s$ as follows:\n\n$$\ns=\\sqrt[3]{V}=\\sqrt[3]{1\\,000}=10\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of a side is $10\\,\\text{in}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539022 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538896 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539022 1069 "q-100773" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539022 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539022 1071 "Solve the equation ${log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}+7)={log}_{2}⁡(2{x}^{2}+8).$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539022 1072 #uuid "ecdce78b-6ad1-5fcc-9999-f701ae74931a" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539022 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}+7) & ={log}_{2}⁡(2{x}^{2}+8) \\\\ {x}^{2}+7 & =2{x}^{2}+8 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${x}^{2}=-1$ has no real solutions. Therefore, our original logarithmic equation has no solutions either." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539023 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538897 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539023 1069 "q-61833" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539023 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539023 1071 "![](images/7a/7a1491a141cde19434464ee6ca7c57a5214aa75ddf26e189bb05b756a68b96da.png)\n\nWhich of the following is a polyhedron with exactly $4$ faces?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539023 1072 #uuid "ee11909f-e3aa-5f76-82e6-f3bec1db8a65" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539023 1074 "Let's examine the solids one-by-one.\n\n- I  is a polyhedron and it has exactly $6$ faces.\n\n- II  is a polyhedron and it has exactly $5$ faces.\n\n- III is a polyhedron but it has exactly $4$ faces.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"III only\"." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539024 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538899 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539024 1069 "q-269516" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539024 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539024 1071 "Consider the function $f:R\\to {R}^{4},$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x?$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539024 1072 #uuid "ee17a4e4-1d8d-5373-a5f7-48bfde731f73" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539024 1074 "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:R\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a scalar $x$ is the column vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂x}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{m\\times 1}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function \n\n- acts on a scalar $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- outputs a vector $f(x)\\in {R}^{4}$ defined by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}x+2 \\\\ 3x \\\\ 4x-10 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{4}}{∂x}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+2) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-10) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(6)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{4\\times 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539025 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538898 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539025 1069 "q-74588" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539025 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539025 1071 "![](images/cb/cb0fa52f4f74145ee88e73f3f4c62d96d6b8261533844b4c8021d30cae8e41d0.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a rectangle?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539025 1072 #uuid "ee602985-c91d-54be-9eef-bda0850e7e34" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539025 1074 "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a rectangle is a quadrilateral where all angles are right and has two pairs of parallel sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is an isosceles trapezoid. It has only one pair of parallel sides, not two, therefore, it is not a rectangle.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a parallelogram. It has no right angles, therefore, it is not a rectangle.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a rhombus. Since it has no right angles, it is not a rectangle.\n\n- The shape $S$ is a rectangle since all its angles are right.\n\nTherefore, shape $S$ is a rectangle. The shapes $P,$ $Q,$ and $R$ are not rectangles." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539026 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538821 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539026 1069 "q-215802" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539026 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539026 1071 "If $x=2,$ then $|x+1{|}^{2}-|x-1{|}^{3}+2=${{field-1}}." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539026 1072 #uuid "f4d3cdfc-97df-54f6-9ee5-ecd71be4d0bd" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539026 1074 "We substitute $x=2$ and then evaluate the absolute values before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+1{|}^{2}-|x-1{|}^{3}+2 & = \\\\ |2+1{|}^{2}-|2-1{|}^{3}+2 & = \\\\ |3{|}^{2}-|1{|}^{3}+2 & = \\\\ {3}^{2}-{1}^{3}+2 & = \\\\ 9-1+2 & = \\\\ 10 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539027 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538841 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539027 1069 "q-118573" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539027 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539027 1071 "The volume of a cube is $\\frac{8}{27}\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}.$ Find the length of the edges of the cube." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539027 1072 #uuid "f8ed5ffa-b9ea-5302-ac51-cfa35a2f9ca0" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539027 1074 "Since the volume of a cube is \n\n$$\nV={s}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the length of an edge, we can find $s$ as follows:\n\n$$\ns=\\sqrt[3]{V}=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{8}{27}}=\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the edges is $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{yd}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539028 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538852 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539028 1069 "q-97780" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539028 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539028 1071 "$\\int \\frac{4}{|x+1|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2x+5}}\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539028 1072 #uuid "fa581feb-4c4c-5d1e-ad5b-763e0315938f" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539028 1074 "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x+5 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}+5 \\\\  & =(x+1)^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{|x+1|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2x+5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{4}{|x+1|\\sqrt{4+(x+1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2}{|\\frac{x+1}{2}|\\sqrt{4+4{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|\\frac{x+1}{2}|\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{x+1}{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{|\\frac{x+1}{2}|\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(\\frac{x+1}{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539029 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538825 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539029 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538830 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539029 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538879 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539029 1069 "q-270275" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539029 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539029 1071 "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}^{3} \\\\ -{x}_{1}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$-3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539029 1072 #uuid "fcdd0ff0-12c7-557b-8715-01260bbcae30" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539029 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{2}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}(2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}(2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2{y}_{2} \\\\ 2 & 3{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}0-{1}^{3} \\\\ -1\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(-{x}_{1}{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(-{x}_{1}{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}^{2} & 1 \\\\ -{x}_{2} & -{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539030 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538877 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539030 1069 "q-81874" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539030 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539030 1071 "![](images/b9/b974b6e782801fbe2099971afd0df9a5c6e05a10afc2ce0e4054c850088e753b.png)\n\nWhat is the volume of the cube?" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539030 1072 #uuid "fef83826-7751-5d92-9867-993dd33f1353" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539030 1074 "The volume $V$ of a cube can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nV={s}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the side length of the cube.\n\nSubstituting $s=2\\,\\text{cm}$ into the formula, we get \n\n$$\nV=(2\\,\\text{cm})^{3}=8\\,{\\text{ cm}}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139535898 17592186